1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.369
Welcome to Governing AI Agents,

2
00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.671
built in partnership with Databricks.

3
00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:08.375
In this course, you'll learn how to integrate data governance

4
00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:11.277
into your agent's workflow to ensure the agent can handle data

5
00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.880
safely, securely, and accurately.

6
00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:18.118
Data governance consists of a set of policies and practices

7
00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:20.820
that you can implement to ensure your agent has access

8
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.123
only to the data that it needs,

9
00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:27.227
that personal information is protected from unauthorized access,

10
00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:30.597
and that you and your team can monitor the agent's performance.

11
00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:34.601
I'm delighted that the instructor for this course is Amber Roberts,

12
00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:38.104
who is staff technical marketing manager at Databricks.

13
00:00:38.405 --> 00:00:40.807
Thanks Andrew, I'm excited to work with you on this course.

14
00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:43.076
Say you want to build an agent

15
00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:45.078
specialized in customer analysis.

16
00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:47.580
You can define for the agent a set of tools

17
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.084
that allow it to ingest data like customer demographics or transactions

18
00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:53.787
or website engagements and survey responses.

19
00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:57.590
If you give this agent broad permissions to all this data,

20
00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.061
it risks the agent leaking private customer

21
00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:04.664
information such as credit card info or their address

22
00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.834
or their personal purchase behavior. Data that shouldn't be visible

23
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.737
to maybe all company employees.

24
00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:15.041
If, however, you built this agent with data governance in mind,

25
00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:18.244
then you can ensure that your agent properly handles this data.

26
00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:21.815
For example, you can specify in the deployment environment.

27
00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:24.517
what tables or columns your agent can access,

28
00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:28.521
and encrypt custom IDs and mask credit card information.

29
00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.825
You can also implement data quality checkpoints

30
00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.327
for the agent's input and output

31
00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:37.530
and add evals to measure output quality.

32
00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.632
And enable observability

33
00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:41.634
to log the agent's processing steps.

34
00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:44.304
All of these practices help you

35
00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:46.806
to continuously monitor the agent's behavior

36
00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.808
and debug any failure scenarios.

37
00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:53.113
In this course, you will learn how to apply these governance practices

38
00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:55.982
to an agent that you'll build and deploy on Databricks.

39
00:01:55.982 --> 00:02:00.086
Now, in order to make sure your agent follows least privilege access,

40
00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:04.090
you'll give the agent specific, intentional access with views.

41
00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.027
These views which are SQL queries that act like tables,

42
00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.830
will contain only the data needed for the agent's task.

43
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.833
Now, for the agent to access these views,

44
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:17.237
you will need to learn how to specify the agent's permissions.

45
00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:20.740
You will then build tools so the agent can access the data

46
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.943
and register those tools as functions in Unity Catalog.

47
00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:27.247
Unity Catalog is an open source data catalog

48
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.484
that ensures only authorized agents or users can access those tools.

49
00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:34.487
After that, you will implement the agent logic

50
00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:37.891
using the OpenAI SDK, evaluate it

51
00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:40.693
and enable tracing using MLflow.

52
00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:42.996
And finally, you will deploy your agent.

53
00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:44.898
To follow along with the course labs,

54
00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:48.168
you can create your account with Databricks free edition

55
00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.069
and try the labs at no cost.

56
00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:52.939
Many people have worked to create this course.

57
00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:54.941
I'd like to thank from Databricks,

58
00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:57.210
Nicolas Pelaez and Ben Wilson.

59
00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.212
From DeepLearning.AI,

60
00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:02.215
Hawraa Salami also contributed to this course.

61
00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:05.418
In the next lesson, Amber will walk you through the four pillars

62
00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.420
of data governance for AI agents,

63
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.225
which summarizes the main aspects of how to govern your AI agents.

64
00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:16.229
The four pillars are: lifecycle management, risk management,

65
00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.932
security, and observability.

66
00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:22.936
So, let's go on to the next video to get started.
